; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Random Walk
; CATEGORY: Science & Simulation
; LEVEL:    Beginner
; TAGS:     random, probability, walk, color
; SUMMARY:  A turtle wanders by picking north, south, east or west at random until it escapes a big circle.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   The turtle starts in the middle of a circle. At every step it picks
;   one of four directions at random and walks a short way. Its trail
;   changes color as time goes on, so you can see where it went first
;   (red) and where it went later (purple). When it finally crosses the
;   circle, the program tells you how many steps it took.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Then run it again: every walk is different! Compare how
;   many steps each walk needed.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * RANDOM to make choices, and PICK to choose from a list
;   * DISTANCE to measure how far the turtle is from a point
;   * UNTIL loops that stop when something becomes true
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Make the circle bigger. Does doubling the size double the steps?
;     (Scientists found it takes about four times as many.)
;   * Allow diagonal moves by picking from [0 45 90 135 180 225 270 315].
;   * Take a bigger step each time and see how the picture changes.
; ============================================================

make "radius 200
make "stride 10

to draw.circle
  ; ARC draws a circle around the turtle without moving it, so the
  ; turtle can stay at home, right where the walk will start.
  penup home
  setpc 15 setpensize 2
  arc 360 :radius
end

; The colors go red, orange, yellow, green, blue, purple, then repeat.
; Every 40 steps the trail moves on to the next color.
to color.for :n
  output item 1 + remainder int (:n / 40) 6 [4 14 6 2 1 13]
end

to wander
  localmake "steps 0
  pendown setpensize 2
  until [(distance [0 0]) > :radius] [
    setpc color.for :steps
    setheading pick [0 90 180 270]
    fd :stride
    wait 1
    make "steps :steps + 1
  ]
  print (sentence [The turtle escaped after] :steps [steps.])
  print (sentence [If it had walked in a straight line it would have needed only] ~
    round :radius / :stride [steps.])
end

clearscreen
setbackground 0
draw.circle
setshape "turtle showturtle
wander
hideturtle
